meta {
  name: 9d4. Verify Named-API Sub-path Resolves
  type: http
  seq: 26
}

get {
  url: {{apisixApiUrl}}/v1/datasets/{{sagaDataSetId}}/{{sagaSlug}}/Things
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{sagaApiAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("SensorThings sub-resource resolves through the named API (200)", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("Response is the SensorThings collection document", function() {
    // FROST answers a collection request with an object carrying a `value` array.
    expect(res.body).to.be.an('object');
    expect(res.body.value).to.be.an('array');
  });
}

docs {
  **Sub-resources of a named API resolve through the same route.**

  Requests `/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}/Things` — a SensorThings collection below the named API's root — with
  the same `api-access` bearer. The saga provisions each named-API route with two URIs, the exact path
  `/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}` **and** the prefix `/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}/*`, so every sub-resource of the
  standard (`/Things`, `/Datastreams`, `/Observations`, …) is served by the same route, the same upstream
  and the same authorization decision. A route provisioned only for the exact path would 404 here and the
  named API would be useless for anything but its service document.

  **Precondition:** `sagaApiAccessToken` set (step 9d2); step 9d3 proved the named API's root is readable, so
  OPA propagation has already settled — no extra poll is needed here.

  **Expected Response:** 200 OK with the FROST SensorThings `Things` collection.

  **Post-condition:** none — read-only.
}
